  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe southern Great Lakes and Upper Ohio Valley regions will\nremain locked in the familiar weather pattern through early next\nweek. An Omega Block, characterized by a couple of mid/upper\ntroughs and closed lows rotating into the Rockies and Four\nCorners regions and another trough/closed low set up off the\nEast Coast with sharp central CONUS ridging in between, will\ngradually break down through Monday. This will allow strong\nCanadian high pressure at the surface, currently centered over\nsouthern New England, to drift south into the Mid Atlantic and\nsouthern Appalachians by Monday. Another night of clear skies\nand light winds with the high nearby tonight will allow lows to\ndip into the upper 30s/low 40s, with some low 30s in far NE Ohio\nand NW PA. Patchy frost is possible in those areas away from the\nlake, but it will be of minimal impact since the growing season\nhas already ended up there. The bigger impact will be areas of\nfog again tonight and Sunday night, but increasing SE return flow\nto mitigate a daily lake breeze will start to lower dew points \nenough to limit fog coverage at night. Expect decent fog \ncoverage in NW Ohio and interior parts of NE Ohio/NW PA again \ntonight since sufficient low-level moisture and small temp/dew \npoint spreads will remain below the inversion, but coverage will\nbe less than last night and will further trend less Sunday\nnight.\n\nA mid/upper shortwave dropping through the eastern Great Lakes\nMonday will drive a weak cold front through the area Monday\nafternoon, but very limited moisture return will lead to a dry \nfrontal passage. The main impact will be a brief cool down, with\nhighs in the upper 60s/low 70s Monday cooling into the upper\n50s/mid 60s Tuesday as a new Canadian high builds down across\nthe Great Lakes. However, starting Wednesday and on into next \nweekend, a strong subtropical Pacific jet extension (a response \nto the powerful El Nino) will drive a series of storm systems\ninto the West Coast. This will build an expanding mid/upper \nridge over the eastern CONUS leading to a big warming trend.\nWidespread 70s are possible the second half of the week and \ninto next weekend, with continued dry conditions until a cold \nfront possibly approaches late Saturday.",
